<h3 id="cc1">1. One canonical form; surface text is a view</h3>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>What it established</th><th>Where it stops</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Intentional Programming, Simonyi, 1995 [C1]</td><td>A program is a tree of nodes, each an instance of an intention; users edit the tree and view it in any notation; computational intent is kept separate from implementation</td><td>A research system at Microsoft; the report describes no determinism or replay model</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unison [C2]</td><td>Each definition is identified by a hash of its syntax tree; names are separately stored metadata</td><td>The hash is of structure: two definitions with the same behaviour and different structure have different hashes</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Equality saturation and egg [C3; C4]</td><td>An e-graph holds many equivalent versions of a program at once; a cost model picks one</td><td>A compiler technique for terms, not a whole-program form</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tacit, 2026 [C5]</td><td>The AST is the source of truth; every valid AST has one byte-exact canonical text; definitions are addressed by BLAKE3 hash; malformed code becomes typed Hole nodes</td><td>One target (LLVM); no state-machine semantics stated</td></tr>
    <tr><td>X07, 2026 [C6]</td><td>Canonical source is a JSON AST, edited with JSON Patch; a 646-code diagnostic catalogue with enforced quickfix coverage</td><td>Canonical bytes, not canonical behaviour</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Codifide, 2026 [C7]</td><td>The canonical form is a typed hypergraph with a deterministic byte form and SHA-256 hash; surface syntax is a projection; intent is part of a definition's identity</td><td>Described by its authors as a contract-and-dispatch language, not general computation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>NAIL [C8]</td><td><code>nail canonicalize</code> normalizes any program; <code>nail check --strict</code> rejects non-canonical input</td><td>An RFC 8785-inspired JSON subset that, in its own words, does not claim full RFC 8785 compliance; byte canonicalization only</td></tr>
    <tr><td>EqNet, Allamanis et al., 2017 [C9]</td><td>Neural representations trained so that semantically equivalent expressions map together</td><td>Algebraic and Boolean expressions, not programs</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

<h3 id="cc2">2. A program is a deterministic state machine over a log</h3>
<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"><table class={tbl}>
  <thead><tr><th>Project</th><th>What it established</th><th>Where it stops</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Abstract State Machines, Gurevich, 2000 [C10]</td><td>The paper's title states the result: sequential abstract state machines capture sequential algorithms</td><td>A theorem, not a system: every sequential algorithm can be simulated step for step by a sequential abstract state machine (Main Theorem 6.13)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Event sourcing, Fowler, 2005 [C11]</td><td>"Capture all changes to an application state as a sequence of events"</td><td>A pattern; determinism and projections are left to each implementation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The Elm Architecture [C12]</td><td>Model, View, Update: state, a view of the state, and an update from messages</td><td>One language and one target (the browser)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Dedalus and the CALM theorem, Hellerstein, Alvaro et al. [C13; C14]</td><td>Datalog with explicit time; programs with consistent, coordination-free distributed implementations are exactly those expressible in monotonic logic</td><td>Research languages; no cross-language emission</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Durable execution: Temporal, Restate [C15; C16]</td><td>Temporal: workflow code "must be deterministic to support replay". Restate: every step and its result are recorded in a journal</td><td>Replay serves resilience, not program identity</td></tr>
    <tr><td>TigerBeetle deterministic simulation testing, 2026 [C17]</td><td>Logical determinism (like FoundationDB) plus physical determinism: replicas converge to byte-by-byte identical state; real code runs in a simulator</td><td>One database, built by hand to this discipline</td></tr>
    <tr><td>AILANG; agent-scroll [C18; C19]</td><td>AILANG: every construct has deterministic semantics, with replayable execution and structured traces. agent-scroll: canonical, byte-deterministic, hash-chained transcripts with a verifier that catches any single-byte change</td><td>AILANG is functional, not a state-machine form; agent-scroll logs conversations, not programs</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

